<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Loire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Loire is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 1,000 km long. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> and flows north and then west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> to the Atlantic at Saint-Nazaire. In antiquity many Gallic peoples lived along it, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a frontier and a highway. Its valley, lined with royal châteaux, is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/loire.jpg" alt="Loire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cramos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Loire_%C3%A0_La_Bohalle_par_Cramos.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
